   "source": [
    "### 2.2. Load the dataset <a id=\"eda_2\"></a>"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "abalone_df = pd.read_csv('Documents/Datasets/abalone.csv')\n",
    "abalone_df.head() # Shows the first five samples"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "&emsp;The dataset on abalone by Nash et al. (1995) was chosen due to several reasons. Firstly, in machine learning, particularly data analysis where datasets can range up to millions of samples, our chosen dataset is considered relatively moderate with a sample size of 4177, making it applicable in real-world scenarios. Secondly, there are eight different features, including one categorical feature, Type, and seven numeric features: Length, Diameter, Height, Whole height, Shucked height, Viscera height, and Shell height. The diversity in the number and dimensions makes the dataset satisfactory to the proposed regression models.\n",
    "\n",
    "**Catagorical Feature:**\n",
    "+ `Type`: Type of abalones (M = male, F = female and I = infant)\n",
    "\n",
    "**Numerical Features:**\n",
    "+ `Length`: Longest shell measurement in mm (continuous)\n",
    "+ `Diameter`: Perpendicular to length in mm (continuous)\n",
    "+ `Height`: with meat in shell in mm (continuous)\n",
    "+ `Whole weight`: whole abalone in g (continuous)\n",
    "+ `Shucked weight`: weight of meat in g (continuous)\n",
    "+ `Viscera weight`: gut weight (after bleeding) in g (continuous)\n",
    "+ `Shell weight`: after being dried in g (continuous)\n",
    "+ `Rings`: +1.5 gives the age in years (discrete)"
   ]
  },
